  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nLike the past couple mornings, a few areas of patchy fog are being \nseen in area observations, however it is not widespread due to the \nscattered upper level clouds situated over the region. This should \nremain the case through the rest of the morning, with any patchy fog \nburning off within a couple hours of sunrise. The rest of today will \nbe beautiful by November standards as a weak boundary slides across \nthe area, resulting in increasing temperatures followed by surface \nhigh pressure later in the day. Much of the area should end up in \nthe 60s during the early afternoon even with cloud cover moving \nacross the area associated with the weak frontal boundaries, with \nmainly western WI staying in the upper 50s as the initial weak warm \nfront arrives a bit later and eats into peak heating hours. The cold \nfront will swing through shortly after the warm front later this \nevening as temperatures cool back into the 30s tonight as surface \nhigh pressure moves in afterwards, clearing skies and allowing for \nmore efficient radiational cooling. Tuesday looks to be a bit cooler \nwith highs generally in the low to mid 50s, but skies will remain \nmostly clear thanks to the high pressure. \n\nTuesday night into Wednesday morning will be the main 'active' \nperiod within the forecast window as 500mb falling heights will \nresult in weak mid to upper level omega as a surface low begins to \nform in response to the eastern side of an approaching upper level \nridge. Given the lack of overall moisture and the fact the omega \nwill have to compete with upper level cold air advection, the chance \nfor showers will be confined mainly to early Wednesday morning with \nlittle to no accumulation expected beyond a few hundredths of an \ninch, with the vast majority of the area remaining dry. The upper \nlevel ridge continues to move eastwards, with strong surface high \npressure setting up as the axis moves overhead and the ridge begins \nto widen over the CONUS. We will remain on the cooler side (at least \ncompared to how warm we have been) as temperatures look to drop \nbelow freezing both Thursday and Friday mornings before rebounding \nback by the end of the weekend. The longer range guidance shows \nlittle in the way of active weather through the middle of the month \nas this ridge remains overhead, and it isn't until we approach the \nmiddle to end of next week to see the ridge begin to break down and \nthe chance for active weather to increase. Until that time, expect \nseasonally moderate temperatures and no precipitation.",
